// # What it is
//
// The "do I have this permission?" self-check interface, the equivalent of Android's `checkSelfPermission`:
// before calling a method that needs a sensitive permission, an app asks first, requests it if not
// (permission.ui's RequestPermission), and calls once it has it.
//
// # Why it has to be a separate interface_id
//
// It can't be a method on `permission.admin`: that interface's interface-level bar is
// `perm.permission.admin` itself (SYSTEM_ONLY + platform-release signature), so ordinary apps
// can't even Resolve it. And self-checking is exactly what **every app must be able to do**.
//
// In the Catalog's model one interface_id maps to a set of endpoint candidates, and one interface_id
// can only have one bar; different bars need two interfaces. The naming follows the camera /
// camera.config shape: two faces of the same domain.
//
// # Why no required_permission
//
// **Self-checking isn't a privilege.** It only answers "do I myself have X", and the caller is entitled to know its own
// authorisation state anyway: it can always try calling that method and get the same answer from
// PERMISSION_DENIED, only at much greater cost (a call doomed to fail,
// and for methods with side effects you can't try it that way at all).
//
// The consequence of adding a permission: if that permission were USER_CONSENT itself, you'd get the loop "you need the permission
// to check whether you have permissions"; if it were NORMAL, everyone would have it, and adding it would only
// put one more line of noise in every app's manifest.
//
// # Boundary: you can only check yourself
//
// The request has **no package_id field**, on purpose. The target package is always the caller itself, which the kernel takes from
// the Dispatch's caller (internal/endpoint's BuiltinCall.Caller, the identity recognised by SO_PEERCRED,
// not something self-reported in the request).
//
// With a package_id field it would be a "check any package's grant state" interface, which belongs to
// permission.admin (`perm.permission.admin` + SYSTEM_ONLY) and shouldn't exist without a bar.
// The permission layout is reconnaissance: knowing which app on which machine has the camera is directly valuable
// to an attacker.
